  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 136 PM CST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nThe surface ridge axis that's overhead this afternoon will gradually \nshift east into MN this evening. As this occurs, southerly winds and \nincreasing warm air advection will be developing/spreading east \nacross the region as a warm front approaches. By 06Z tonight, 850mb \ntemps should range from about +13C to +16C across the CWA. Cold \nfront follows the warm front fairly quickly and temps aloft are \nalready cooling by Monday morning. That said, the air mass is still \nrather mild even post-frontal and highs still look to be well above \nnormal, reaching the upper 50s to low 60s for most areas. \n\nWill then be watching the wave of low pressure that moves southeast \nacross the area Tuesday/Tuesday night. Models are trying to generate \nlight QPF with this system as it moves through. Inherited NBM PoPs \nhighlight the eastern CWA with slight chances Tuesday evening. The \nENS camp of solutions is more bullish with probability of measurable \nprecipitation (0.01in), showing around 50-75% across the eastern \nCWA. Probabilities for measurable stretch further west over central \nSD as well, so will have to see if slight/low chances for precip get \nexpanded further westward across the CWA over the next few forecast \ncycles.\n\nModels continue to show a brief shot of colder air following this \nsystem, especially by Thursday. Grand Ensemble 850mb temps seem to \nbe a tad colder than they were 24 hours ago, now showing temps aloft \nas cold as -3C to 0C across the eastern CWA. The 12Z deterministic \nGFS run is as cold as -4C over the eastern CWA at 18Z Thursday. \nInherited NBM high temps are only in the upper 30s across the \neastern CWA for Thursday. As previously mentioned though, this cold \nlooks to be short lived as models bring back 850mb temps of +10C or \nwarmer next weekend, and should lead to above normal readings once \nagain.",
